[[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] entered the tournament in the third round, as a [[Premier League]] team. They began with a 5–0 home victory over [[Huddersfield Town A.F.C.|Huddersfield Town]] at the [[City of Manchester Stadium|Etihad Stadium]] with two goals for City from [[Phil Foden]], one from [[Julián Álvarez]], an [[own goal]] from [[Ben Jackson (footballer, born 2001)|Ben Jackson]] and [[Jérémy Doku]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rose |first=Gary |date=7 January 2024 |title=Manchester City 5–0 Huddersfield |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/67895100 |access-date=20 April 2024}}</ref> They then defeated [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] away in the fourth round in a 1–0 victory with a sole goal for City from [[Nathan Aké]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=26 January 2024 |title=Tottenham Hotspur 0–1 Manchester City |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/68097502 |access-date=20 April 2024}}</ref> In the fifth round, they beat [[Luton Town F.C.|Luton Town]] 6–2 away at [[Kenilworth Road]] with five goals from [[Erling Haaland]] and one from [[Mateo Kovačić]] and two goals for Luton from [[Jordan Clark (English footballer)|Jordan Clark]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Drury |first=Sam |date=27 February 2024 |title=Luton Town 2–6 Manchester City |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/68417647 |access-date=20 April 2024}}</ref> 

In the quarter-final match, Manchester City hosted [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] at the Etihad Stadium where they came out 2–0 winners in the end which put them through to the semi-finals with two goals for City from [[Bernardo Silva]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=16 March 2024 |title=Manchester City 2–0 Newcastle United |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/68573294 |access-date=20 April 2024}}</ref> In the semi-final match, held at the neutral venue of [[Wembley Stadium]], Manchester City defeated [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in a 1–0 victory to qualify for a second consecutive FA Cup final with the sole goal for City from Silva.<ref>{{Cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=20 April 2024 |title=Manchester City 1–0 Chelsea |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/68860454 |access-date=20 April 2024}}</ref> This was the third time that City played in consecutive FA Cup finals, having previously done so in the [[1933 FA Cup final|1933]] and [[1934 FA Cup final|1934]] as well as [[1955 FA Cup final|1955]] and [[1956 FA Cup final|1956]] finals.

