As a Premier League team, [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] also entered the tournament in the third round. They began their campaign with a 2–0 away win over [[EFL League One|League One]] side [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]] with goals from [[Diogo Dalot]] and a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] from [[Bruno Fernandes]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Emons |first=Michael |date=8 January 2024 |title=Wigan 0–2 Man Utd: Man Utd see off League One Wigan in FA Cup |work=BBC Sport |url= https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/67914807 |access-date=21 April 2024}}</ref> before recording a 4–2 away win over [[EFL League Two|League Two]] side [[Newport County A.F.C.|Newport County]] at [[Rodney Parade]]. Fernandes scored the opener, before [[Kobbie Mainoo]] scored his first goal for United. Newport then managed to bring the tie level with goals from [[Bryn Morris]] and [[Will Evans (footballer, born 1997)|Will Evans]], but goals from [[Antony (footballer, born 2000)|Antony]] and [[Rasmus Højlund]] secured United's passage to the next round.<ref>{{Cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=28 January 2024 |title=Newport County 2–4 Man Utd: Manchester United avoid upset at Rodney Parade |work=BBC Sport |url= https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/68110828 |access-date=21 April 2024}}</ref> In the fifth round, United travelled to the [[City Ground]] and defeated [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]] 1–0, with [[Casemiro]] heading a late winner.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stone |first=Simon |date=28 February 2024 |title=Nottingham Forest 0–1 Manchester United: Casemiro scores winner to book last-eight spot |work=BBC Sport |url= https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/68428925 |access-date=21 April 2024}}</ref>

In the quarter-finals, United faced rivals [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] at [[Old Trafford]]; this was only time in the FA Cup campaign that they were drawn at home. [[Scott McTominay]] gave United the lead within 10 minutes, before [[Alexis Mac Allister]] and [[Mohamed Salah]] scored within minutes to Liverpool a 2–1 lead going into half-time. United looked like they were going out of the FA Cup at the hands of their rivals at home for the first time in over 100 years, but [[Antony (footballer, born 2000)|Antony]] scored a late-equaliser to send the match to extra time. Before the interval, [[Harvey Elliott]] gave Liverpool the lead again, but [[Marcus Rashford]] made it 3–3. In the final minutes of extra time, [[Amad Diallo]] scored the winning goal. Amad was given a yellow second card and sent off for taking his shirt off in celebration, having picked up a yellow card earlier in the match.<ref>{{Cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |date=17 March 2024 |title=Manchester United 4–3 Liverpool (aet): Amad Diallo scores dramatic extra-time winner to send hosts into semis |work=BBC Sport |url= https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/68573308 |access-date=21 April 2024}}</ref>

In the semi-final match, also held at Wembley, United faced [[Coventry City F.C.|Coventry City]], who were the last side not from the top-flight still in the competition. Scott McTominay, [[Harry Maguire]] and Bruno Fernandes gave United a comfortable 3–0 lead, which they squandered, with goals from [[Ellis Simms]], [[Callum O'Hare]], and a stoppage-time penalty from [[Haji Wright]] which sent the match into extra time. In the final minute of extra time, [[Victor Torp]] looked to have completed a miraculous comeback and sent Coventry to the final, but the goal was ruled out by [[Video assistant referee|VAR]] in a moment of controversy. In the [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shootout]], United defeated Coventry 4–2, with Højlund scoring the winning penalty to set up a [[Manchester derby]] final for a second successive season.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stone |first=Simon |date=21 April 2024 |title=Coventry City 3–3 Manchester United (aet; 2–4 on pens): Man Utd scrape past Coventry to reach FA Cup final |work=BBC Sport |url= https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/68428925 |access-date=21 April 2024}}</ref> This was the sixth time that United had played in two successive FA Cup finals and the first time since [[2004 FA Cup final|2004]] and [[2005 FA Cup final|2005]].{{cn|date=January 2026}}

==Pre-match==
[[The Football Association]] confirmed that the 2024 final would begin at 15:00, the same time as the previous FA Cup final did, as agreed by the [[Metropolitan Police]].<ref name="FA KO TV money">{{cite web |date=23 April 2024 |title=The Emirates FA Cup Final Will Take Place At Wembley At 3pm On Saturday 25 May 2024 |url=https://www.thefa.com/news/2024/apr/22/emirates-fa-cup-final-2023-24-20242204 |access-date=25 May 2024 |website=[[The Football Association]] |location=London}}</ref>

Prior to the match, considerable attention was focused on the future of Manchester United manager [[Erik ten Hag]], as his side finished 8th in the Premier League, the club's lowest position since its formation in 1992. Reports indicated that the club had decided to sack him regardless of the final result.<ref name="SkySports">{{cite news|last=Yew|first=Oliver|url=https://www.skysports.com/football/man-city-vs-man-utd/report/504674|title=Man City 1-2 Man Utd: Erik ten Hag's side end season on high with stunning FA Cup final win at Wembley|date=26 May 2024|access-date=26 May 2024|website=Sky Sports}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Steinberg|first=Jacob|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/article/2024/may/24/manchester-united-decide-to-sack-erik-ten-hag-regardless-of-cup-final-outcome|title=Manchester United decide to sack Erik ten Hag regardless of Cup final outcome|newspaper=The Guardian|date=24 May 2024|access-date=26 May 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/c033gxr2w7lo|title=Erik ten Hag sack rumours: Manchester United manager prepares for FA Cup final amid reports of firing|first=Simon|last=Stone|website=BBC Sport|date=24 May 2024|access-date=26 May 2024}}</ref>

Manchester City were considered as heavy favourites to win the match by the English media; having clinched a record-breaking fourth successive Premier League title the previous week,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Man City make history with fourth title in a row |url=https://www.premierleague.com/news/4019801 |access-date=2025-01-06 |website=www.premierleague.com |language=en}}</ref> a win would make them the first team to win a [[Double (association football)|league and cup double]] in successive seasons and would also make them the first team to retain the FA Cup since [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] in 2015.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Murray |first=Scott |date=2015-05-30 |title=The 2015 FA Cup final: Arsenal 4-0 Aston Villa, as it happened |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2015/may/30/arsenal-v-aston-villa-fa-cup-final-live |access-date=2025-01-06 |work=the Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> City had beaten United in both league fixtures between them, winning 3–0 at [[Old Trafford]]<ref>{{Cite news |date=2023-10-27 |title=Manchester United 0-3 Manchester City: Erling Haaland and Phil Foden secure derby victory |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/67185669 |access-date=2025-01-06 |work=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref> and 3–1 at the [[City of Manchester Stadium|Etihad Stadium]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2024-03-01 |title=Man City 3-1 Man Utd: Phil Foden scores twice as home side come back to win Manchester derby |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/68395537 |access-date=2025-01-06 |work=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref> Meanwhile, a win for United would secure European football, having failed to qualify due to their league position.

===Broadcasting===
The final was shown live on [[BBC One]], [[BBC iPlayer]], [[ITV1]], [[ITVX]], [[UTV (TV channel)|UTV]] and [[STV (TV channel)|STV]].<ref name="FA KO TV money" /> Live text commentary was available on the [[BBC Sport]] website and app.<ref>{{Cite web |title=FA Cup Final TV Guide |url=https://soccersat.com/news/Where-to-watch-Man-City-vs-Man-United-in-FA-Cup-Final |access-date=2024-05-24 |website=soccersat.com}}</ref> The BBC coverage was led by [[Gary Lineker]] alongside [[Wayne Rooney]] and [[Micah Richards]], with the match commentary provided by [[Guy Mowbray]] and [[Alan Shearer]].<ref name="BBC broadcast">{{cite web | date=25 May 2024 |title=Men's FA Cup Final 2024 - How to watch Manchester City v Manchester United on TV and BBC |url=https://www.bbc.com/mediacentre/articles/2024/mens-fa-cup-final-manchester-city-v-manchester-united |website=BBC |access-date=25 May 2024}}</ref> The ITV coverage was led by [[Mark Pougatch]], who was joined by [[Karen Carney]], [[Roy Keane]] and [[Ian Wright]]; their commentary team consisted of [[Sam Matterface]], [[Lee Dixon]] and [[Ally McCoist]], with [[Gabriel Clarke]] providing interviews. National radio commentary was provided by [[BBC Radio 5 Live]] and [[Talksport]],<ref>{{cite web |first=James |last=Walters |date=23 May 2024 |title=What channel is Man City v Man Utd FA Cup final match on? TV coverage, live stream and kick-off time |url=https://www.radiotimes.com/tv/sport/football/man-city-man-utd-fa-cup-final-2023-24-preview/ |website=RadioTimes.com |access-date=25 May 2024}}</ref> while [[BBC Radio Manchester]] covered the match for local radio.<ref>{{cite web |title=BBC Radio Manchester - Manchester Total Sport: Commentary, Manchester City v Manchester United |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/p0hxrq95 |website=BBC |access-date=25 May 2024}}</ref>

===Match officials===
The match officials were confirmed by the FA on 8 May 2024 with [[Andrew Madley]] ([[West Riding County Football Association|West Riding County football association]]) acting as the referee who previously refereed [[FA Trophy]] finals and [[FA Vase]] finals in his career. He was joined by assistant referees Harry Lennard ([[Sussex County Football Association|Sussex County football association]]) and Nick Hopton ([[Derbyshire County Football Association|Derbyshire County football association]]) with the fourth official being Simon Hooper ([[Wiltshire Football Association|Wiltshire football association]]) and the reserve assistant referee was Tim Wood ([[Gloucestershire County Football Association|Gloucestershire County football association]]). The video assistant referee was [[Michael Oliver (referee)|Michael Oliver]] ([[Durham County Football Association|Durham County football association]]), the support VAR was [[Peter Bankes]] ([[Liverpool County Football Association|Liverpool County football association]]) and the assistant VAR was Stuart Burt ([[Northamptonshire Football Association|Northamptonshire football association]]).<ref name="officials"/>

==Match==
===Summary===
[[File:FA Cup Final.jpg|thumb|[[Wembley Stadium]] during the 2024 FA Cup final between [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] and [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]].]]

The match kicked off at 15:00 in front of 84,814 fans. [[Marcus Rashford]] was through on goal immediately, but [[Kyle Walker]] managed to beat the United forward to the ball. Manchester City then attacked, and [[Lisandro Martínez]] made contact with [[Erling Haaland]] in the penalty area. City appealed for a penalty, but the appeal was dismissed by the referee. Six minutes later, [[Bruno Fernandes]] found [[Alejandro Garnacho]] on the right-hand side of the penalty area, before his shot was saved comfortably by [[Stefan Ortega]]. In the 30th minute, Garnacho opened the scoring for United after a mistake at the back as [[Joško Gvardiol]] looked to head a deep pass from Diogo Dalot back to Ortega, who had come off his line too far, which enabled the United forward to pass it into an empty net. United had the ball in the back of the net again as Garnacho teed up Rashford, who guided the ball at the back post, but it did not count and the assistant referee had his flag up. In the 39th minute, Rashford played a ball over the top to Garnacho on the right-hand side, who picked out Fernandes in the centre, and the United captain played a first-time pass to Kobbie Mainoo who burst into the penalty area and scored to double United's lead.<ref name="SportingNews">{{cite web|last=Cox|first=Brad|url=https://www.sportingnews.com/us/soccer/news/man-city-man-united-fa-cup-final-updates-live-score-result-stats-lineups/25f79bc609845b204a7e7c42|title=Man City vs Man United FA Cup final score, result, stats as Garnacho and Mainoo give Ten Hag famous triumph|work=Sporting News|date=26 May 2024|access-date=26 May 2024}}</ref>

In the 55th minute, substitute [[Jérémy Doku]] played a pass into the penalty area which fell to Haaland, who struck the ball off the crossbar. City continued their pressure, with Walker's effort from outside the penalty area being saved by [[André Onana]]. City came close again, as substitute Julián Álvarez scuffed his effort wide after a one-on-one against Onana. With three minutes to play, Doku received the ball on the left-hand side and shifted it onto his right before shooting towards the bottom corner from just outside the penalty area into the bottom left-hand corner of the net to pull one back. However, United held on after seven minutes of stoppage time and the match ended 2–1 in their favour.<ref name="SportingNews"/>

